 window.vishStudyData=Object.assign({},window.vishStudyData||{},{"english":[{"major":1,"units":[{"unit":"1","title":"LITERATURE AND ITS TYPES / FORMS","cards":[{"q":"From which Latin word does the word “literature” come, and what does it mean?","a":"The word “literature” comes from the Latin word “Littera”, meaning “letter”."},{"q":"According to Padmaja Ashok, what are the two parts of literature?","a":"Content (what is said) and Form (how it is said), These are the two parts of literature"},{"q":"Who described literature as a “criticism of life”?","a":"Matthew Arnold described literature as a “criticism of life”"},{"q":"Which writer gave the idea of “committed” literature in What is Literature?","a":"Jean-Paul Sartre."},{"q":"State any two characteristics of literature.","a":"(1) Literature is imaginative and emotional. (2) Universal and timeless; a reflection of life and society."},{"q":"What is a myth? Give one example of mythological literature.","a":"A myth is a traditional story about gods, heroes, the creation of the world and natural forces, which explains the beliefs of a people. Example: Greek mythologies."},{"q":"What is a Fable? Name one Greek and one Sanskrit collection of fables.","a":"A fable is a short story conveying a moral, in which the characters are animals with human qualities. 1)Greek: Aesop’s fables, 2) Sanskrit: the Panchatantra / Hitopadesha."},{"q":"What is an Ovi?","a":"An Ovi is a form of Marathi folk verse, traditionally sung by women while grinding grain or doing household work. It has short rhymed lines."},{"q":"What is an Abhang?","a":"An Abhang is devotional poetry in Marathi composed by the Varkari saints and addressed to Lord Vitthal of Pandharpur."},{"q":"Name the four main forms of literature.","a":"Poetry, Drama, Fiction and Non-fiction."},{"q":"What is canonical literature?","a":"Works regarded as the best and most lasting in a literature and generally taught in schools and universities, e.g. Shakespeare, Milton, Austen and Dickens."},{"q":"What is science fiction?","a":"Fiction based on imagined scientific or technological developments, such as space travel and time machines."}]},{"unit":"2","title":"MINOR AND MAJOR GENRES OF ENGLISH LITERATURE","cards":[{"q":"From which Greek word does “poetry” come, and what does it mean?","a":"Greek word “poetry” came from the Greek poietes, It means “creator”."},{"q":"What is a ballad? From which Latin word does the term come?","a":"A ballad is a song that narrates a story. The term comes from the Latin “Ballare”. It means “to dance”."},{"q":"What is an epic? Name one primary and one secondary epic.","a":"An epic is a long narrative poem on the exploits of great heroes, in a grand style. Primary: the Ramayana (or Iliad, Beowulf), Secondary: Milton’s Paradise Lost (or Virgil’s Aeneid)."},{"q":"What is a lyric?","a":"A short non-narrative poem expressing a mood or feeling; it was originally sung to the lyre."},{"q":"What is an ode? Give one example.","a":"A serious, elevated and longer lyric addressed to a person, object or idea, with formal stanzas. Example: Keats’s “Ode on a Grecian Urn”."},{"q":"What is an elegy? Name a famous elegy by Gray.","a":"An elegy is a poem of lament for the dead or for a tragic event. Gray’s “Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard” (1751)."},{"q":"How many lines does a sonnet have, and in which country did it originate?","a":"A sonnet has fourteen lines (iambic pentameter). It originated in Italy."},{"q":"Give the rhyme scheme of a Shakespearean sonnet.","a":"abab cdcd efef gg (three quatrains and a closing couplet)."},{"q":"Define drama.","a":"Drama is a form of literature that has plot, characters, dialogue and action, and is meant to be performed on the stage."},{"q":"Define tragedy. Which work of Aristotle describes its features?","a":"Tragedy is a serious action with a disastrous end for the protagonist, who is of noble birth or high rank. Aristotle’s Poetics describes its features."},{"q":"Name the five parts of the plot of a play according to Gustav Freytag.","a":"Exposition, Rising Action, Climax, Falling Action and Dénouement (catastrophe in tragedy)."},{"q":"From which Italian word does “novel” come, and what does it mean?","a":"”Novel” came from the Italian word “Novella”, meaning “a new thing” or “tale”."},{"q":"Who was the first important writer to analyse the short story form?","a":"Edgar Allan Poe."},{"q":"Define a one-act play.","a":"A dramatic performance that has only one act. It is to drama what the short story is to the novel."},{"q":"Who is called the father of the essay form?","a":"Michel de Montaigne, the French Renaissance philosopher."},{"q":"From which Greek words does “biography” come?","a":"”Biography” came from bios (“life”) and graphein (“to write”)."},{"q":"Who first used the term “autobiography” in the sense of one’s own life story?","a":"Robert Southey, in 1809."},{"q":"How does a memoir differ from an autobiography?","a":"A memoir is an account of the author’s personal experiences focused on a particular period, person or events; an autobiography covers the whole life in order."}]}]},{"major":2,"units":[{"unit":"1","title":"THEORY OF SHORT STORY","cards":[{"q":"Define the short story.","a":"A short piece of fiction that focuses on a single character (or very few characters), a single incident, a single impression or the creation of a single effect. It can be read in one sitting."},{"q":"What is meant by “economy of expression” and “tightness of structure”?","a":"Economy of expression means no wasted words; tightness of structure means every part of the story is closely connected to the whole."},{"q":"Name the Sanskrit collection of stories from India of about the third century BCE.","a":"The Panchatantra."},{"q":"Who wrote The Decameron?","a":"Giovanni Boccaccio, the Italian writer (a collection of a hundred tales)."},{"q":"Who wrote The Canterbury Tales? How many stories does the book contain?","a":"Geoffrey Chaucer; it contains twenty-four stories."},{"q":"In which century was the short story recognised as a major literary form, and in which country did it first become popular?","a":"The nineteenth century; it first became popular in America."},{"q":"Who was the first important author to analyse the short story form?","a":"Edgar Allan Poe."},{"q":"What is a fable? Give one example.","a":"A short story conveying a moral, in which the characters are animals with human qualities. Example: Aesop’s fables / the Hitopadesha."},{"q":"What is a fabliau?","a":"A short, satirical or comic tale in verse, associated with medieval French literature and known for its bawdiness."},{"q":"What is an exemplum? Name a literary example.","a":"An anecdote with a moral, used especially in sermons. Example: Chaucer’s “The Pardoner’s Tale”."}]},{"unit":"2","title":"SHORT STORIES","cards":[{"q":"Name Ruskin Bond’s first novel and the prize it won.","a":"The Room on the Roof (1956). It won the John Llewellyn Rhys Prize in 1957."},{"q":"For which work did Ruskin Bond receive the Sahitya Akademi Award, and in which year?","a":"Ruskin Bond receive the Sahitya Akademi Award for “Our Trees Still Grow in Dehra”, in 1992."},{"q":"Who narrates the story The Eyes Are Not Here?","a":"A blind young man narrates it in the first person."},{"q":"At which station does the girl board the train, and at which station does she get down?","a":"She boards at Rohana and gets down at Saharanpur."},{"q":"What does the narrator describe to the girl about Mussoorie in October?","a":"Wild dahlias, delicious sunshine, log-fires at night, and quiet, almost deserted roads."},{"q":"What does the new passenger reveal about the girl at the end?","a":"That she was completely blind."},{"q":"What kind of irony is used in the ending of the story?","a":"Situational irony (with dramatic irony on a second reading); a surprise ending in the manner of O. Henry."},{"q":"Name any two themes of the story.","a":"Blindness and perception; deception and self-deception. (Others: loneliness, pride and dignity, appearance versus reality.)"},{"q":"THE GIFT OF THE MAGI – O. Henry Short Answer Questions (1 Mark Each) 1. What is the real name of O. Henry?","a":"William Sydney Porter."},{"q":"What is the “O. Henry ending”?","a":"A sudden twist in the last lines that reverses the reader’s expectation."},{"q":"How much money does Della have at the beginning of the story?","a":"One dollar and eighty-seven cents ($1.87)."},{"q":"What are the two treasures of the Youngs?","a":"Jim’s gold watch (inherited from his father and grandfather) and Della’s long brown hair."},{"q":"To whom does Della sell her hair, and for how much?","a":"To Madame Sofronie, for twenty dollars."},{"q":"What gift does Jim buy for Della?","a":"A set of tortoise-shell combs, side and back, with jewelled rims."},{"q":"How does Jim pay for Della’s combs?","a":"He sells his gold watch."},{"q":"Who are the Magi?","a":"The wise men who brought gifts to the infant Jesus and are said to have invented the art of giving Christmas presents."}]}]}],"psychology":[{"major":1,"units":[{"unit":"1","title":"PSYCHOLOGY – INTRODUCTION","cards":[{"q":"What is psychology?","a":"Psychology is the scientific study of behaviour and mental processes. It examines what people and other animals do, as well as processes such as thinking, feeling, learning and remembering."},{"q":"Define psychology as a science.","a":"Psychology is a science because it uses systematic observation, measurement and research to study behaviour and mental processes. It tests explanations against evidence and revises conclusions when findings do not support them."},{"q":"What is behaviour?","a":"Behaviour refers to observable actions and responses of people or animals, such as speaking, moving or showing a facial expression. Psychologists study behaviour to describe it and understand the processes behind it."},{"q":"What are mental processes?","a":"Mental processes are internal activities of the mind that cannot be observed directly, including thinking, feeling, remembering and perceiving. Psychologists infer them from a person’s behaviour and responses."},{"q":"State any one goal of psychology.","a":"One goal is description: psychologists carefully observe and record what behaviour occurs, who shows it, and under what conditions. The four broad goals are description, explanation, prediction and control."},{"q":"What is structuralism?","a":"Structuralism was an early approach that sought to identify the basic elements and structure of conscious experience. Researchers used introspection, asking trained observers to describe sensations and feelings in response to stimuli."},{"q":"Who is associated with structuralism?","a":"Wilhelm Wundt established the first psychology laboratory and is often called the founder of experimental psychology. His student Edward Titchener developed structuralism and analysed conscious experience into basic elements."},{"q":"What is functionalism?","a":"Functionalism studies how mental activities and behaviour help an organism adapt to its environment. Influenced by evolutionary theory, it asks what a thought or action does rather than breaking consciousness into separate elements."},{"q":"Who is associated with functionalism?","a":"William James is most closely associated with functionalism in the United States. He examined the functions of consciousness and how mental processes help people adapt to everyday demands."},{"q":"What is Gestalt psychology?","a":"Gestalt psychology emphasizes that perception is organized into meaningful wholes rather than experienced as separate elements. The mind groups sensations into patterns, so the whole experience can differ from the sum of its parts."},{"q":"What is psychoanalysis?","a":"Psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud’s theory of personality and method of therapy. It proposes that unconscious wishes and conflicts influence behaviour, and that bringing them into awareness can help explain psychological difficulties."},{"q":"Who developed psychoanalysis?","a":"Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis from his clinical work with patients. His theory emphasized unconscious processes, early childhood experiences and conflicts among parts of personality."},{"q":"Define behaviourism.","a":"Behaviourism is an approach that focuses on observable behaviour and how it is learned through experience. Behaviourists sought explanations based on measurable responses and environmental events rather than introspection about consciousness."},{"q":"Who is associated with behaviourism?","a":"John B. Watson is associated with founding behaviourism as a movement focused on observable behaviour. B. F. Skinner later developed influential work on operant conditioning and the effects of consequences."},{"q":"What is humanistic psychology?","a":"Humanistic psychology emphasizes personal choice, free will, growth and the capacity to develop one’s potential. Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers were major figures; they viewed people as more than products of unconscious conflict or conditioning."},{"q":"What is cognitive psychology?","a":"Cognitive psychology studies how people acquire, process, store and use information. It investigates mental activities such as attention, perception, memory, language, reasoning and problem-solving, often by examining observable performance."},{"q":"Define biopsychology.","a":"Biopsychology studies the biological bases of behaviour and mental processes. It examines how the brain, nervous system, hormones and genes contribute to actions, emotions and cognition."},{"q":"What is evolutionary psychology?","a":"Evolutionary psychology considers how psychological tendencies may have developed because they helped ancestors survive or reproduce. It looks for adaptive functions of behaviour while recognizing that learning and culture also shape people."},{"q":"What is clinical psychology?","a":"Clinical psychology focuses on assessing, understanding and treating psychological disorders and significant emotional or behavioural difficulties. Clinical psychologists may provide therapy, conduct research and use assessment methods in clinical settings."},{"q":"What is educational psychology?","a":"Educational psychology applies psychological principles to learning and teaching. It studies how students learn, classroom behaviour, assessment and the effects of instruction, helping educators understand educational needs and improve learning conditions."},{"q":"What is social psychology?","a":"Social psychology studies how people’s thoughts, feelings and behaviour are influenced by other people and social situations. Topics include attitudes, conformity, prejudice, cooperation, conflict and group behaviour."},{"q":"What is developmental psychology?","a":"Developmental psychology studies changes and continuities across the lifespan. It examines physical, cognitive, emotional, personality and social development from infancy through old age, and the factors that shape these changes."},{"q":"What is positive psychology?","a":"Positive psychology studies human strengths and conditions that support well-being, such as resilience, hope, creativity and positive relationships. It complements the study of psychological problems by asking how people can flourish."},{"q":"What is the scientific approach in psychology?","a":"The scientific approach gathers evidence systematically to reduce bias and error. Psychologists formulate questions, collect observable data, analyse results and use the findings to evaluate explanations rather than relying only on intuition."},{"q":"Define hypothesis.","a":"A hypothesis is a tentative, testable explanation or prediction about a phenomenon. It specifies a relationship that can be examined through observation or research, and may be supported or rejected by the evidence."},{"q":"What is naturalistic observation?","a":"Naturalistic observation is a descriptive method in which researchers watch and record behaviour in its usual setting without deliberately changing the situation. It can reveal behaviour as it occurs in everyday life."},{"q":"What is a case study?","a":"A case study is an in-depth investigation of one person, group or unusual situation. Researchers combine detailed information from sources such as interviews, records and observations, though findings may not generalize to everyone."},{"q":"What is a survey method?","a":"A survey collects information by asking people questions, commonly through questionnaires or interviews. It can gather reports from many participants efficiently, but answers may be affected by memory, wording or willingness to respond accurately."},{"q":"What is critical thinking?","a":"Critical thinking is the careful evaluation of claims and evidence before accepting a conclusion. It involves asking what supports an idea, considering alternative explanations and remaining willing to revise a belief when stronger evidence appears."}]},{"unit":"2","title":"COGNITIVE PROCESSES","cards":[{"q":"Define sensation.","a":"Sensation is the process by which sensory receptors detect physical energy from the environment or body and convert it into neural signals. These signals provide the basic input that the brain can then organize and interpret."},{"q":"What is transduction?","a":"Transduction is the conversion of physical energy, such as light, sound or pressure, into neural activity by sensory receptors. This makes information from the environment available for processing by the nervous system."},{"q":"What is a sensory receptor?","a":"A sensory receptor is a specialized cell or nerve ending that responds to a particular form of stimulation, such as light, vibration, pressure or chemicals. It begins the process of converting that energy into neural signals."},{"q":"What is an absolute threshold?","a":"An absolute threshold is the minimum intensity of a stimulus that a person can detect at least 50 percent of the time under specified conditions. Detection can vary with attention, fatigue and the surrounding noise."},{"q":"What is a difference threshold?","a":"A difference threshold, or just noticeable difference, is the smallest change between two stimuli that a person detects at least 50 percent of the time. The size of the change needed often depends on the original stimulus intensity."},{"q":"What is sensory adaptation?","a":"Sensory adaptation is the reduced sensitivity of sensory receptors after continuous exposure to an unchanging stimulus. For example, a persistent smell may become less noticeable, allowing attention to shift toward new or changing information."},{"q":"What is habituation?","a":"Habituation occurs when the brain gradually stops attending to a repeated or constant stimulus, even though sensory receptors may continue responding. It reduces attention to familiar input so that changes and significant events stand out."},{"q":"What is attention?","a":"Attention is the selective focusing of awareness on some information while other input is filtered or receives less processing. It helps determine which sensory information receives further processing and may enter working memory."},{"q":"Define perception.","a":"Perception is the process of organizing and interpreting sensory information so that it becomes meaningful. Unlike sensation, which detects stimulation, perception identifies patterns, objects and events using both sensory input and experience."},{"q":"What is perceptual organization?","a":"Perceptual organization is the brain’s grouping of sensory elements into meaningful patterns and objects. Gestalt principles such as figure-ground, proximity, similarity and closure help explain how separate parts are perceived as organized wholes."},{"q":"What is figure-ground organization?","a":"Figure-ground organization is the tendency to distinguish a focal object, or figure, from the background against which it appears. The same visual display can sometimes be organized in more than one way, changing which part seems to be the figure."},{"q":"What is the Gestalt principle of proximity?","a":"Proximity is the tendency to perceive elements that are near one another as belonging to the same group. For example, closely spaced dots are usually seen as a row or cluster rather than as unrelated points."},{"q":"What is the Gestalt principle of similarity?","a":"Similarity is the tendency to group elements that share features such as colour, shape or size. When similar items appear among different ones, people commonly perceive the similar items as forming a pattern or group."},{"q":"What is the Gestalt principle of closure?","a":"Closure is the tendency to perceive an incomplete figure as complete by mentally filling in missing parts. It allows people to recognize familiar shapes even when portions of their outlines are not shown."},{"q":"What is depth perception?","a":"Depth perception is the ability to perceive distance and the three-dimensional layout of objects. The brain combines visual information, including monocular and binocular cues, to judge how far away objects are and how they relate in space."},{"q":"What is a monocular cue?","a":"A monocular cue is a visual indication of depth that can be used with one eye. Examples include relative size, interposition, linear perspective, texture gradient and height in the visual field."},{"q":"What is a binocular cue?","a":"A binocular cue is a depth cue that depends on information from both eyes. Retinal disparity compares the slightly different images received by each eye, while convergence uses the inward turning of the eyes for nearby objects."},{"q":"What is a perceptual illusion?","a":"A perceptual illusion is an interpretation of a real stimulus that differs from its physical properties or actual arrangement. Illusions show that perception is an active interpretation and can be influenced by context and expectations."},{"q":"Define learning.","a":"Learning is a relatively lasting change in behaviour or knowledge that results from experience. It is distinguished from temporary changes caused only by fatigue, illness or maturation, and may occur through association, consequences or observation."},{"q":"What is classical conditioning?","a":"Classical conditioning is learning an association between stimuli, so that a previously neutral stimulus comes to elicit a response. In Pavlov’s example, a sound paired repeatedly with food eventually produced salivation on its own."},{"q":"What is operant conditioning?","a":"Operant conditioning is learning in which the consequences of a voluntary response affect how likely it is to occur again. Reinforcement strengthens behaviour, whereas punishment makes behaviour less likely."},{"q":"What is observational learning?","a":"Observational learning is learning by watching another person’s behaviour and its consequences. The learner may later imitate the model, especially when the behaviour is remembered, can be reproduced and appears rewarded."},{"q":"What is reinforcement?","a":"Reinforcement is any consequence that increases the likelihood of the response it follows. Positive reinforcement adds a desirable outcome, while negative reinforcement removes an unpleasant condition; both strengthen behaviour."},{"q":"What is punishment?","a":"Punishment is a consequence that decreases the likelihood of the response it follows. It may add an unpleasant event or remove something desirable, and differs from negative reinforcement, which increases behaviour."},{"q":"What is the law of effect?","a":"Thorndike’s law of effect states that responses followed by satisfying consequences become more likely, while responses followed by unpleasant consequences become less likely. It helped establish the importance of consequences in learning."},{"q":"What is latent learning?","a":"Latent learning is learning that occurs without an immediate reward and is not shown until there is a reason to use it. It suggests that learning can take place even when performance does not change right away."},{"q":"What is insight learning?","a":"Insight learning is the sudden recognition of a solution or of relationships among parts of a problem. The solution may appear without a long sequence of visible trial-and-error responses, as in Köhler’s studies of chimpanzees."},{"q":"What is learned helplessness?","a":"Learned helplessness is a pattern in which repeated experience of having no control leads a person or animal to stop trying to escape or change a later situation. It can reduce effort even when a solution becomes possible."},{"q":"Define memory.","a":"Memory is the active system by which information is encoded, stored and later retrieved. It involves more than retaining a record: attention, organization and cues affect what is kept and how successfully it can be recalled."},{"q":"Name the three processes of memory.","a":"The three processes are encoding, storage and retrieval. Encoding changes information into a usable form, storage retains it over time, and retrieval brings stored information back into awareness when it is needed."},{"q":"What is encoding?","a":"Encoding is the process of transforming incoming information into a form that can be stored in memory. Attention and the way information is organized or connected to prior knowledge can affect how well it is encoded."},{"q":"What is storage?","a":"Storage is the retention of encoded information over time. Information may be held briefly or maintained in long-term memory, and its durability can depend on rehearsal, meaningful organization and later experience."},{"q":"What is retrieval?","a":"Retrieval is the process of accessing information held in memory and bringing it into a usable form. Recall and recognition are two forms of retrieval, and both can be helped or hindered by available cues."},{"q":"What is sensory memory?","a":"Sensory memory is the brief initial holding of raw sensory information after stimulation ends. It preserves a momentary trace, such as a visual image or sound, giving attention a short opportunity to select information for further processing."},{"q":"What is short-term memory?","a":"Short-term memory temporarily holds information that has received attention, often for only seconds unless it is rehearsed. Its capacity is limited, so information may be lost, displaced or transferred through rehearsal and elaboration."},{"q":"What is working memory?","a":"Working memory is an active system that briefly holds and manipulates information while a person performs a task. It supports activities such as following instructions, solving a problem and combining new information with what is already known."},{"q":"What is long-term memory?","a":"Long-term memory is the relatively enduring store of information, knowledge and experiences. It has a very large capacity, and retrieval depends on how information was encoded, organized and cued."},{"q":"What is recall?","a":"Recall is retrieval in which a person must produce previously learned information with few or no answer choices. Answering an essay question from memory is an example; cues can make recall easier."},{"q":"What is recognition?","a":"Recognition is identifying information as familiar or previously encountered. Selecting a correct answer in a multiple-choice test is an example, and it usually provides more external cues than recall."},{"q":"What is the curve of forgetting?","a":"The forgetting curve, first studied by Ebbinghaus, shows that memory for newly learned material often declines rapidly at first and then more slowly. Rehearsal and meaningful learning can reduce the amount forgotten."},{"q":"What is encoding failure?","a":"Encoding failure occurs when information is not processed well enough to enter memory. Because it was not adequately stored in the first place, a person may be unable to retrieve it later even though it seemed familiar."},{"q":"What is interference?","a":"Interference is forgetting caused when memories compete with or disrupt one another. Proactive interference occurs when older learning disrupts newer learning; retroactive interference occurs when newer learning disrupts retrieval of older information."},{"q":"What is proactive interference?","a":"Proactive interference occurs when previously learned information makes it harder to remember or learn newer information. For example, an old password may come to mind when trying to recall a recently changed password."},{"q":"What is retroactive interference?","a":"Retroactive interference occurs when newer learning makes it harder to retrieve information learned earlier. For example, learning a new phone number may make the old number more difficult to recall."}]}]},{"major":2,"units":[{"unit":"1","title":"MOTIVATION AND EMOTION","cards":[{"q":"Define motivation.","a":"Motivation is the process that starts, directs and sustains activity toward meeting physical or psychological needs and wants. It helps explain why behaviour begins, what goal it follows and how long it continues."},{"q":"What is intrinsic motivation?","a":"Intrinsic motivation comes from interest or enjoyment inherent in an activity. A person who reads because the subject is satisfying is intrinsically motivated, without needing an external reward to continue."},{"q":"What is extrinsic motivation?","a":"Extrinsic motivation comes from an outcome separate from the activity, such as money, praise or avoiding punishment. The activity is performed as a means to gain a reward or prevent an unwanted consequence."},{"q":"What is instinct?","a":"An instinct is an innate, biologically based pattern of behaviour that appears in a species without requiring prior learning. Instinct theories historically tried to explain motivation through inherited action tendencies."},{"q":"What is a need?","a":"A need is a requirement or desire that creates a condition of deficiency or imbalance. It may be biological, such as the need for food, or psychological, such as the need for belonging or achievement."},{"q":"What is a drive?","a":"A drive is a state of tension or arousal produced by an unmet need that energizes behaviour. In drive-reduction theory, behaviour is directed toward satisfying the need and reducing this tension."},{"q":"What is drive-reduction theory?","a":"Drive-reduction theory proposes that a biological need creates an uncomfortable drive that motivates behaviour to restore balance. For example, thirst prompts drinking, which reduces the drive and supports the body’s equilibrium."},{"q":"What are primary drives?","a":"Primary drives arise from biological needs necessary for survival, such as hunger, thirst and avoidance of pain. They are not learned in the same way as secondary drives, though experience can shape how they are expressed."},{"q":"What are acquired or secondary drives?","a":"Acquired, or secondary, drives develop through learning and experience rather than directly from a biological need. Money, achievement or social approval can become motivating because they are associated with rewards or valued goals."},{"q":"What is homeostasis?","a":"Homeostasis is the body’s tendency to maintain a relatively stable internal state. When a condition such as blood glucose or temperature shifts away from its range, physiological processes and motivated behaviour help restore balance."},{"q":"What is the need for affiliation?","a":"The need for affiliation is the desire for friendly relationships, acceptance and connection with others. People high in this need seek approval and companionship and often value cooperation and harmonious group relationships."},{"q":"What is the need for power?","a":"The need for power is the desire to influence, control or have an impact on other people and events. In McClelland’s account, it is a learned psychological need and differs from simply achieving a personal goal."},{"q":"What is the need for achievement?","a":"The need for achievement is the desire to accomplish challenging goals and meet a standard of excellence. People high in this need often prefer tasks with meaningful responsibility and feedback about their performance."},{"q":"What is arousal theory?","a":"Arousal theory proposes that people seek a level of alertness or stimulation that is satisfying to them. Performance is often best at a moderate arousal level, while very low or high arousal can interfere with a task."},{"q":"Who are sensation seekers?","a":"Sensation seekers are people who prefer varied, novel and intense experiences and may accept some risk to obtain them. Their choices reflect a stronger desire for stimulation rather than simply the reduction of a biological need."},{"q":"What is an incentive?","a":"An incentive is an external goal or event that attracts or repels behaviour. Its anticipated value can motivate action, such as working toward a reward or avoiding a negative outcome."},{"q":"What is incentive theory?","a":"Incentive theory explains motivation through the pull of external stimuli and anticipated consequences. Unlike drive reduction, which emphasizes internal tension, it highlights how rewards and other goals draw behaviour."},{"q":"What is self-actualization?","a":"Self-actualization is the realization of one’s potential and the pursuit of personal growth. In Maslow’s theory it is a higher-level need, generally pursued as more basic needs are sufficiently met."},{"q":"What are peak experiences?","a":"Peak experiences are moments of intense fulfillment, joy or deep engagement that Maslow associated with self-actualization. They may involve a sense of meaning or unity and are described as especially vivid and satisfying."},{"q":"What is Self-Determination Theory?","a":"Self-Determination Theory explains motivation through needs for autonomy, competence and relatedness. When people feel choice, effectiveness and connection, they are more likely to show self-directed and sustained motivation."},{"q":"Define emotion.","a":"Emotion is a feeling state that involves bodily arousal, expressive behaviour and a conscious experience or interpretation. Emotions influence action and communication, and can affect relationships, health and decision-making."},{"q":"Name the three elements of emotion.","a":"The three elements are physiological arousal, outward expressive behaviour and subjective conscious experience. Together they include bodily changes, visible actions such as facial expression, and the person’s felt and labelled emotion."},{"q":"What happens physiologically during an emotion?","a":"During emotion, the autonomic nervous system can change heart rate, breathing, sweating and muscle readiness. These bodily responses prepare action, while the brain processes the situation and the person becomes aware of the feeling."},{"q":"What is the James-Lange theory of emotion?","a":"The James-Lange theory proposes that a stimulus first produces bodily changes, and awareness of those changes is experienced as emotion. In simplified form, a person feels afraid because they notice their body trembling."},{"q":"What is the Cannon-Bard theory of emotion?","a":"The Cannon-Bard theory proposes that an emotional stimulus produces bodily arousal and the conscious experience of emotion at about the same time. The feeling is not simply caused by first noticing a bodily response."},{"q":"What is the facial feedback hypothesis?","a":"The facial feedback hypothesis proposes that facial muscle activity can influence or contribute to emotional experience. For example, adopting an expression associated with an emotion may affect how strongly that emotion is felt."}]},{"unit":"2","title":"PERSONALITY AND INTELLIGENCE","cards":[{"q":"Define personality.","a":"Personality is the distinctive and relatively consistent pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving that characterizes an individual. It develops through the interaction of inherited tendencies, personal experience and social or cultural influences."},{"q":"What is temperament?","a":"Temperament refers to biologically based tendencies in emotional reaction and behaviour that appear early in life. It describes characteristic differences such as activity level, adaptability and emotional intensity, which experience can further shape."},{"q":"What is a personality trait?","a":"A personality trait is a relatively stable characteristic that describes how a person tends to think, feel or behave across situations. Traits help summarize individual differences but do not determine every action in every context."},{"q":"What are trait theories of personality?","a":"Trait theories describe personality through relatively enduring characteristics that differ among people. Researchers identify and measure traits to summarize patterns of behaviour and compare individuals, rather than explaining personality mainly through unconscious conflicts."},{"q":"What are surface traits according to Cattell?","a":"Cattell’s surface traits are clusters of observable behaviours that appear together in everyday life. They are visible descriptions of personality, while deeper source traits are inferred as underlying factors that help produce those patterns."},{"q":"What are source traits according to Cattell?","a":"Source traits are underlying dimensions that Cattell believed generate related patterns of observable behaviour. Through factor analysis he identified 16 source traits, which formed the basis of the 16PF personality questionnaire."},{"q":"What is the 16PF?","a":"The 16PF, or Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire, is an assessment developed from Cattell’s 16 source traits. It measures several personality dimensions and is used to describe individual differences through structured responses."},{"q":"What is the Big Five model?","a":"The Big Five model organizes personality into five broad trait dimensions found across many studies: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism. Each dimension describes a range rather than a fixed type of person."},{"q":"Name the five dimensions of the Big Five.","a":"The five dimensions are openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism. Together they provide a broad framework for describing differences in curiosity, organization, sociability, cooperation and emotional stability."},{"q":"What is openness?","a":"Openness describes curiosity, imagination and willingness to consider new ideas and experiences. People higher on this dimension tend to be more exploratory, while lower scores are often associated with preference for familiarity and convention."},{"q":"What is conscientiousness?","a":"Conscientiousness describes organization, dependability and self-discipline. People who score higher often plan ahead, work carefully and persist toward goals; lower scores can reflect a more spontaneous or less structured style."},{"q":"What is extraversion?","a":"Extraversion describes sociability, assertiveness and the tendency to seek stimulation in social settings. People higher in extraversion are often outgoing and energetic, while those lower may prefer quieter or more solitary activities."},{"q":"What is agreeableness?","a":"Agreeableness describes a person’s tendency toward cooperation, trust and consideration of others. Higher scores are associated with warmth and helpfulness, while lower scores may reflect greater skepticism or competitiveness."},{"q":"What is neuroticism?","a":"Neuroticism describes the tendency to experience negative emotions and emotional instability. People higher on this dimension may be more prone to worry or moodiness, whereas lower scores indicate greater emotional steadiness."},{"q":"What is the unconscious in Freud’s theory?","a":"In Freud’s theory, the unconscious contains wishes, memories and conflicts outside ordinary awareness that can still influence behaviour. Freud believed that these hidden processes may appear indirectly in dreams, slips or emotional symptoms."},{"q":"What is the pleasure principle?","a":"The pleasure principle is the id’s demand for immediate satisfaction of impulses and reduction of tension. It operates without concern for practical consequences, so the ego must help regulate its demands in real situations."},{"q":"What is the ego?","a":"The ego is the part of personality that operates according to the reality principle. It considers the external situation and tries to satisfy the id’s wishes in practical, socially acceptable ways."},{"q":"What is the superego?","a":"The superego represents internalized rules, values and standards learned from parents and society. It evaluates behaviour and can produce pride or guilt, while sometimes conflicting with the id’s impulses and the ego’s realistic plans."},{"q":"What are defence mechanisms?","a":"Defence mechanisms are mostly unconscious strategies the ego uses to reduce anxiety caused by inner conflict. Examples include repression, denial and rationalization; they may protect a person temporarily but can distort awareness of a situation."},{"q":"Name Freud’s five psychosexual stages.","a":"Freud’s five psychosexual stages are oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital. He proposed that each stage focuses on a different erogenous zone and that unresolved conflict at a stage may influence later personality."},{"q":"What is fixation?","a":"Fixation is, in Freud’s theory, a lasting focus on concerns from a psychosexual stage when conflict was not adequately resolved. Freud believed it could shape adult behaviour, although this explanation is not established as a scientific fact."},{"q":"Define intelligence.","a":"Intelligence is the ability to learn from experience, acquire knowledge and use it effectively to adapt to new situations or solve problems. The book also notes that cultures may differ in how they define intelligent behaviour."},{"q":"What is Gardner’s theory of Multiple Intelligences?","a":"Gardner’s theory proposes that intelligence includes several relatively distinct abilities rather than one single capacity. It recognizes different strengths, such as linguistic, musical and interpersonal ability, though the theory remains debated and researched."},{"q":"Name Gardner’s nine intelligences given in the book.","a":"The nine are linguistic, logical-mathematical, musical, spatial, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal, naturalistic and existential intelligence. The book notes that Gardner added the eighth and later proposed the ninth as a tentative category."},{"q":"What is fluid intelligence according to Cattell?","a":"Fluid intelligence is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying heavily on previously learned knowledge. It supports flexible thinking and tends to be especially important when a task is unfamiliar."},{"q":"What is crystallized intelligence according to Cattell?","a":"Crystallized intelligence is the knowledge and skills accumulated through education and experience. It includes learned vocabulary, facts and strategies, and can continue to develop as a person acquires information."}]}]}]});
